<!-- Airhub vs Orange Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set airhubBest = providerA.codes | bestValidCode %}
  {% set orangePrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airhub</strong> if you just need cheap data and don't mind SMS-only (no voice calls). Pick <strong>Orange</strong> if you specifically need real phone calls on the go, not just data and texts, and you're fine paying a lot more for a household-name European carrier behind it. These two aren't really competing for the same buyer: Airhub is a budget data-first app, Orange is a full-service telecom bundle.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Data-First Budget App or Full Carrier Bundle</h2>
  <p>
    Airhub sells data in multi-gigabyte blocks at some of the lowest per-GB prices on this site, and bundles in SMS plus a local phone number on most plans, useful for receiving a verification text or handing out a number that isn't your own. What it doesn't include is voice calls: no plan on Airhub lets you place or receive a phone call over the cellular network. Orange Travel, the international eSIM arm of Orange, one of Europe's largest telecom groups, goes the opposite direction: unlimited calls and SMS plus hotspot tethering are bundled into every plan as standard, the one real gap in Airhub's lineup.
  </p>
  <p>
    That gap is exactly what you pay for. Airhub sits in the "Cheap" price bracket on this site; Orange sits in "Expensive." A working discount code narrows that gap slightly on Airhub's side, but nowhere near enough to make the two comparable on price alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Price Snapshot (before any code)</h2>
  <div class="content-box">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Destination</th>
